// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GoBuiltinIntegerArithmeticOverflow — Go's OWN D2a(3) carrier for
// built-in integer overflow on **typed** operands (no shared superset
// with rust.dag). Go 1.26: overflow is silent truncation to the result
// type's width in two's complement representation — there is no panic
// branch for ordinary `+ - * /` on typed integers (unlike Rust's
// debug-default panic). The signed / unsigned split mirrors Go's
// **asymmetric** width vocabulary (`uintptr` exists only on the unsigned
// path — see `UnsignedIntWidth` vs `SignedIntWidth` above); BOTH
// variants denote the **same** runtime wrap semantics per spec — the
// tag records which spec partition the operand type inhabits, not a
// second overflow mechanism.
//
// Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Integer_overflow
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every consumer of Go typed-int overflow
//      semantics reads this tag uniformly; shared classifier.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: `{ signed: Bool }` duplicates kernel
//      `Bool` and reopens K-1 string-as-tag; the Disj IS the partition.
//   3. Algebraic form — FAILS: wrap is not a homomorphism into a richer
//      std/ lattice; it is Go-runtime primitive dispatch.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: exactly one signedness class applies per
//      typed integer operation; mutually exclusive.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: not `F<X>` over a separate width
//      enum (Practice 7); width already lives on the GoScalar / alias row.
// Terminal: the closed two-way partition aligning overflow disposition
// with Go's signed-vs-unsigned type taxonomy at the same grain as the
// scalar width carriers.
type GoBuiltinIntegerArithmeticOverflow
  = SignedTypedIntegerTruncatesSilentlyOnOverflow
  | UnsignedTypedIntegerTruncatesSilentlyOnOverflow


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GoIntegerOverflowDisposition<IRCarrier> — per-alias D2a(3) record for
// Go integer types once std/integer.dag lands (🟡 TRACKED-SCAFFOLD
// ledger below). Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger on the record).
//
// Go has **no** debug-vs-release overflow split for these built-ins.
// Both fields therefore carry the **same** `GoBuiltinIntegerArithmeticOverflow`
// variant for a landed row (producer obligation — debug_default ==
// release_default). The two-field shape preserves a uniform read-side
// contract for downstream lenses that already key off debug/release
// axes for other targets without importing Rust's semantic fork.
//
// Spec: https://go.dev/ref/spec#Integer_overflow
//
// Per the D2 row D2.5: overflow vs arbitrary-precision is still selected
// by the std/ alias target once the ladder exists; this record is only
// Go's side of the operation-semantics story.
type GoIntegerOverflowDisposition<IRCarrier> {
  debug_default: GoBuiltinIntegerArithmeticOverflow
  release_default: GoBuiltinIntegerArithmeticOverflow
}


